---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---
<BaseLayout
  title="SPF PermError: What It Means and How to Fix It"
  description="SPF permerror explained: what it means when your published records cannot be correctly interpreted, common causes, how it differs from fail and softfail, and safe correction steps."
>
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 py-12">
    <nav class="mb-4 text-sm text-ink-faint" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
      <a href="/" class="hover:text-accent">Home</a> <span aria-hidden="true">/</span>
      <a href="/email-authentication/" class="hover:text-accent">Email Authentication</a> <span aria-hidden="true">/</span>
      <span class="text-ink-dim">SPF PermError</span>
    </nav>
    <h1 class="font-display text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-ink sm:text-4xl">SPF PermError: What It Means and How to Fix It</h1>
    <p class="mt-5 text-lg leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">
      A permerror result means the domain's published records could not be correctly interpreted. Unlike <code class="font-mono text-accent-soft">fail</code> (which is an explicit statement that the sender is not authorized), permerror indicates the policy itself is broken and cannot be evaluated. It is a distinct error result, not a sender-authorization verdict.
    </p>

    <div class="rich-text mt-8">
      <h2>PermError vs. other SPF results</h2>
      <div class="mt-3 overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60">
        <table class="pdns-table">
          <thead><tr><th>Result</th><th>What it means (RFC 7208 §2.6)</th><th>Receiver action</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td class="font-mono text-xs text-pass">none</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">No SPF record found, or no usable identity</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">No SPF assertion to act on</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="font-mono text-xs text-ink-dim">neutral</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">The domain explicitly states no assertion either way</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">No SPF-based decision</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="font-mono text-xs text-pass">pass</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">The client is authorized to send with this identity</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Receiver policy - pass does not guarantee acceptance</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="font-mono text-xs text-warn">softfail</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">A weak statement that the host is probably not authorized</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Receiver policy - may accept, penalize, or other</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="font-mono text-xs text-fail">fail</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">The client is not authorized to use this identity</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Receiver policy - fail does not guarantee rejection</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="font-mono text-xs text-warn">temperror</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">A transient (typically DNS) error during evaluation</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Receiver policy - may retry, accept, or defer</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="font-mono text-xs text-fail font-bold">permerror</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">The published records could not be correctly interpreted</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Receiver policy - permerror is distinct from "none"</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="mt-3 text-xs text-ink-faint">Source: RFC 7208 <a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc7208#section-2.6" class="text-accent hover:text-accent-soft" rel="noopener">§2.6</a>. The "Receiver action" column describes the receiver's decision domain, not a specified outcome.</p>

      <h2>Common causes of permerror</h2>
      <p>The following are common causes, not an exhaustive list. Other conditions in RFC 7208 can also produce this result.</p>

      <h3>1. Multiple SPF records</h3>
      <p>
        RFC 7208 <a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc7208#section-4.5" class="text-accent hover:text-accent-soft" rel="noopener">§4.5</a> requires exactly one <code>v=spf1</code> TXT record per domain. If the record set includes more than one, evaluation terminates with permerror. This commonly happens when two departments each add their own SPF record, or when a new record is added without deleting the old one.
      </p>
      <p class="text-sm text-ink-faint">Synthetic example - two records found for <code class="font-mono">example.com</code>:</p>
      <pre class="mt-2 rounded-lg border border-line bg-panel-2 p-4 font-mono text-xs leading-relaxed text-accent-soft">example.com.  IN TXT  "v=spf1 include:_spf.google.com ~all"
example.com.  IN TXT  "v=spf1 include:sendgrid.net ~all"
→ Result: permerror (multiple records)</pre>

      <h3>2. Syntax errors</h3>
      <p>
        If the record contains terms the evaluator cannot parse - misspelled mechanisms, malformed IP addresses, unrecognizable syntax - evaluation stops with permerror. RFC 7208 <a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc7208#section-4.6" class="text-accent hover:text-accent-soft" rel="noopener">§4.6</a> defines the grammar; anything outside it triggers this result.
      </p>
      <p class="text-sm text-ink-faint">Synthetic example - unrecognized mechanism:</p>
      <pre class="mt-2 rounded-lg border border-line bg-panel-2 p-4 font-mono text-xs leading-relaxed text-accent-soft">v=spf1 include:_spf.google.com inclde:sendgrid.net ~all
                                          ^^^^^^ not a valid mechanism
→ Result: permerror (syntax error)</pre>

      <h3>3. Exceeded DNS lookup limit</h3>
      <p>
        SPF evaluation is limited to <strong class="text-ink">10 DNS-querying terms</strong> across the entire evaluation - including nested includes within included domains. RFC 7208 <a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc7208#section-4.6.4" class="text-accent hover:text-accent-soft" rel="noopener">§4.6.4</a> specifies this limit. The terms that count are: <code class="font-mono">include</code>, <code class="font-mono">a</code>, <code class="font-mono">mx</code>, <code class="font-mono">ptr</code>, <code class="font-mono">exists</code>, and the <code class="font-mono">redirect</code> modifier. (The <code class="font-mono">ptr</code> mechanism counts as a DNS-querying term although RFC 7208 <a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc7208#section-5.5" class="text-accent hover:text-accent-soft" rel="noopener">§5.5</a> recommends against publishing it.) When an evaluation would exceed the limit, it returns permerror.
      </p>
      <p>
        A top-level count of lookup terms in your record is a useful screening tool, but it is incomplete: nested includes can add additional DNS-querying terms during evaluation. Only a recursive evaluation can confirm the total.
      </p>

      <h2>Worked example: recursive lookup counting</h2>
      <p class="text-sm text-ink-faint">
        The following is a self-contained synthetic example. All records and the test sender IP are defined below. You can reproduce the count by following RFC 7208 §4.6.4 and §5 step by step.
      </p>

      <h3>Records</h3>
      <pre class="mt-2 rounded-lg border border-line bg-panel-2 p-4 font-mono text-xs leading-relaxed text-accent-soft">example.com.    IN TXT "v=spf1 include:esp1.example include:esp2.example ~all"

esp1.example.   IN TXT "v=spf1 include:_net.esp1.example ip4:198.51.100.10 ~all"

_net.esp1.example. IN TXT "v=spf1 ip4:198.51.100.0/24 ~all"

esp2.example.   IN TXT "v=spf1 ip4:203.0.113.5 ~all"

Test sender IP: 198.51.100.10</pre>

      <h3>Evaluation trace</h3>
      <pre class="mt-2 rounded-lg border border-line bg-panel-2 p-4 font-mono text-xs leading-relaxed text-accent-soft">check_host(198.51.100.10, example.com):
1. include:esp1.example          → DNS query #1
   check_host(198.51.100.10, esp1.example):
   2. include:_net.esp1.example  → DNS query #2
      check_host(198.51.100.10, _net.esp1.example):
      3. ip4:198.51.100.0/24     → no DNS query (literal IP)
         198.51.100.10 is in 198.51.100.0/24 → MATCH
         → include returns pass
   → esp1.example evaluation stops (include matched)
   → esp1.example returns pass
→ example.com: first include matched → evaluation stops at ~all
→ Result: softfail (reached ~all, no mechanism matched the IP before it)

Wait - re-check: include:esp1.example matched, so the include mechanism
returns pass. Evaluation of example.com's record stops at the next term
(~all is never reached because include already matched).

Correction: include:esp1.example returned pass → example.com returns pass.

Total DNS-querying terms evaluated: 2
(1 for include:esp1.example + 1 for include:_net.esp1.example)
Terms NOT evaluated: include:esp2.example, ~all
(evaluation stopped after the first include matched)

→ Result: pass (authorized)
→ Lookup count: 2 of 10</pre>

      <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-ink-dim">
        Key point: evaluation stops as soon as a mechanism matches. The second <code class="font-mono">include:esp2.example</code> was never queried because the first include already returned <code class="font-mono">pass</code>. If the sender IP were <code class="font-mono">203.0.113.5</code> instead, the first include would not match (esp1's network is 198.51.100.0/24), evaluation would continue to the second include, which would match - still using only 3 DNS queries (include:esp1.example, its nested include, and include:esp2.example).
      </p>
      <p class="mt-2 text-xs text-ink-faint">
        This is a synthetic walkthrough for illustration - it is not output from our checker or any tool. Our <a href="/spf-record-checker/" class="text-accent hover:text-accent-soft">SPF Record Checker</a> counts top-level terms only; it does not recursively evaluate include chains.
      </p>

      <h2>Safe correction steps</h2>
      <ol class="list-decimal space-y-1.5 pl-5">
        <li><strong class="text-ink">Diagnose the cause.</strong> Use the <a href="/spf-record-checker/" class="text-accent hover:text-accent-soft">SPF Record Checker</a> to inspect your record's syntax and count its top-level lookup terms. The tool reports syntax errors and a top-level count that may indicate a problem.</li>
        <li><strong class="text-ink">If multiple records:</strong> merge all mechanisms into a single <code class="font-mono">v=spf1</code> record and delete the duplicate. Every authorized sender must appear in the one remaining record.</li>
        <li><strong class="text-ink">If syntax error:</strong> correct the misspelled or malformed term. The checker's term table shows which term it could not parse.</li>
        <li><strong class="text-ink">If over the lookup limit:</strong> first check whether every <code class="font-mono">include:</code> is still needed. Remove any for services you no longer use. Then check your email provider's current SPF guidance - they may have consolidated or changed their include domains. Only after exhausting these options, consider whether <a href="/spf-flattener/" class="text-accent hover:text-accent-soft">SPF flattening</a> makes sense. Flattening replaces includes with IP addresses, which can become stale if the provider changes their infrastructure - it is a maintenance trade-off, not a default fix.</li>
        <li><strong class="text-ink">Retest.</strong> Run the <a href="/spf-record-checker/" class="text-accent hover:text-accent-soft">SPF Record Checker</a> again. For sender-level verification, send a test message and inspect the <code class="font-mono">Authentication-Results</code> header.</li>
      </ol>

      <h2>Related</h2>
      <ul class="grid gap-2">
        <li><a href="/spf-lookup-limit/" class="text-accent hover:text-accent-soft">The SPF 10-lookup limit explained</a></li>
        <li><a href="/spf-record/" class="text-accent hover:text-accent-soft">SPF records: the complete guide</a></li>
        <li><a href="/spf-record-checker/" class="text-accent hover:text-accent-soft">SPF Record Checker (free tool)</a></li>
        <li><a href="/spf-flattener/" class="text-accent hover:text-accent-soft">SPF Flattener (free tool, with trade-offs)</a></li>
      </ul>

      <p class="mt-6 text-xs text-ink-faint">
        Primary reference: <a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc7208" class="text-accent hover:text-accent-soft" rel="noopener">RFC 7208 - Sender Policy Framework</a>. Synthetic examples are labeled and are for illustration only.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
